rsonal responsibility that he  was humbled and he was sorry and in a  sense you know  he absolutely is  but what worried me was that every  opportunity he seemed to minimize that  he emphasized how brief his visits to  the parties were he defended his own  actions and what was extraordinary is he  said when it came to thanking staff at a  gathering which someone was fined for  and therefore was illegal he didn't  think he'd done anything wrong and  actually he'd do it again and i think  what worries me about that is that it  makes the whole apology seem somewhat  irrelevant like he's some sort of  toddler he's been forced into an apology  he gets caught and he runs off  protesting the unfairness of it all and  where i would disagree with say chris  bryant who you had on a few minutes ago  is that i know i've known boris for a  long time he will be genuinely shocked  and sorry about the rudeness towards  cleaning and security staff so what  steve barkley says is is absolutely true  that's not his bag at all he would have  been genuinely appalled but i think  there's a problem here i think the rest  of it unlike a lot of people he doesn't  see this as the big deal that people are  making out and i think the question we  have to ask ourselves is is why he  thinks that  do you think he knew what was going on  no you're not  you know i i don't think there's  anything  deliberate here i mean i think you'd  have to be fairly blind not to notice  you know music pumping out in the in the  middle of the night or or people going  on longer or frankly  you know bottle strewn everywhere or  vomit on the walls on the other hand  he's running a country  during a pandemic and i think that's the  problem my feeling from reading the  report yesterday and you know listening  to the whistleblowers who were on the  bbc's panorama program the other night  is that downing street unlike the rest  of the country was a bubble of normality  there was no sign whatsoever that any of  the change